Title: Diagnosis

Details:
The test `testPcts()` fails because the method `getPct(Object v)` in the `Frequency` class returns a cumulative percentage rather than the simple percentage of the value `v` as expected by the test.

Reviewing the implementation of `getPct(Object v)`:
```java
@Deprecated
public double getPct(Object v) {
    return getCumPct((Comparable<?>) v);
}
```
This method is marked deprecated and internally calls `getCumPct(Comparable<?>)`, which returns the cumulative percentage (the sum of frequencies for all values less than or equal to `v`), not the percentage of the exact value `v`.

Reviewing the intended correct method, `getPct(Comparable<?>)`:
```java
public double getPct(Comparable<?> v) {
    final long sumFreq = getSumFreq();
    if (sumFreq == 0) {
        return Double.NaN;
    }
    return (double) getCount(v) / (double) sumFreq;
}
```
This method returns the proper percentage of the exact value `v` in the frequency distribution.

The test case asserts that `f.getPct((Object)(Integer.valueOf(3)))` equals 0.5, i.e., expects the percentage of entries exactly equal to 3, not the cumulative percentage up to 3. However, the current deprecated `getPct(Object)` implementation returns the cumulative percentage, leading to the test failure.

The report comments that replacing version 1.2 with 2.0 is not straightforward because all `getPct` calls on `Object` types will yield cumulative percentages without code changes. This is consistent with this bug: the `getPct(Object)` usage breaks expected non-cumulative behavior.

In summary, the cause of the failure is that `Frequency.getPct(Object)` mistakenly calls `getCumPct(Comparable<?>)` instead of `getPct(Comparable<?>)`. The correct behavior would be for `getPct(Object)` either to be removed (deprecated) or to forward to `getPct(Comparable<?>)` instead of `getCumPct(Comparable<?>)`. This mismatch causes the test failure since the test uses `getPct(Object)` and expects a non-cumulative percentage.

Recent bug reports do not add relevant information to this diagnosis.

Hence, the root cause is the incorrect delegation of `Frequency.getPct(Object)` to `getCumPct(Comparable<?>)` instead of the correct `getPct(Comparable<?>)`. The test fails because it uses the deprecated `getPct(Object)`, which does not behave as expected.

Recommended fix locations would involve changing the implementation of `getPct(Object)`.

